/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Beliefs batch Y: traditions, epics and sacred stories */
WB.L("sikhism", "beliefs", "beginner", "Sikhism: the faith of the free kitchen",
  "Walk into any gurdwara in the world and someone will offer you a hot meal, no questions asked. Here is the faith behind that open door.",
  [
    ["A meal for anyone", "Langar, the free communal meal served at every gurdwara, is Sikh equality put into practice.",
      "Picture a vast hall at the Golden Temple in Amritsar, India. Rows of people sit cross-legged on long mats: farmers, tourists, businesspeople, the very poor. Volunteers ladle out dal, vegetables and fresh roti. Nobody pays, and nobody is asked their religion or caste. On a busy day the kitchen serves tens of thousands of meals.\n\nThis is langar, the free community kitchen found at every gurdwara, or Sikh place of worship. Sitting together on the floor is deliberate, because it puts everyone at the same level. For Sikhs, langar is not charity handed down from above. It is a practical way of living out equality."],
    ["Nanak's message", "Guru Nanak taught one formless God, honest work and sharing, and rejected caste and empty ritual.",
      "Sikhism began in Punjab with Guru Nanak, born in 1469. Tradition says he vanished into a river for three days and returned saying, 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim.' Sikhs read this as a call to look past labels to a shared humanity and one God. His teachings rejected empty ritual and the caste system. The word Sikh means learner or disciple.\n\nSikhs believe in one formless God, expressed in the phrase Ik Onkar, 'One Creator'. The aim of life is to move from ego towards God. Three practices help: Naam Japna (remembering God), Kirat Karni (earning an honest living) and Vand Chakna (sharing what you have)."],
    ["Ten Gurus, one book", "After ten human Gurus, the scripture itself, the Guru Granth Sahib, became the eternal Guru.",
      "Guru Nanak was followed by nine more human Gurus over roughly two centuries. The fifth, Guru Arjan, compiled the hymns of the Gurus and of some Hindu and Muslim saints into a single scripture.\n\nBefore his death in 1708, the tenth Guru, Gobind Singh, made a remarkable decision: there would be no more human Gurus. The scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, would be the eternal Guru. It is treated with deep respect. In a gurdwara it rests on a raised platform under a canopy, someone waves a whisk over it, and at night it is carried ceremonially to its own resting room. Visitors cover their heads and remove their shoes."],
    ["The Khalsa and Five Ks", "Initiated Sikhs keep five articles of faith, and shared surnames reject caste.",
      "In 1699, at the spring festival of Vaisakhi, Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa, a community of initiated Sikhs committed to discipline, courage and defending the vulnerable. Members keep the Five Ks: kesh (uncut hair), kara (a steel bracelet), kanga (a small wooden comb), kachera (a cotton undergarment) and kirpan (a small ceremonial blade, a symbol of standing against injustice).\n\nUncut hair is covered by a turban, or dastaar, which many Sikhs see as a commitment to faith and dignity. Many Sikh men take the surname Singh (lion) and many women Kaur (often 'princess'). Shared surnames reject caste, since regional surnames often revealed it."],
    ["Sikhs in Australia", "Sikhs have lived in Australia since the 1800s, and many gurdwaras take langar to disaster-hit communities.",
      "Sikhs are not newcomers to Australia. In the 1800s, Punjabi men worked here as hawkers and farm labourers, travelling the bush selling goods from carts. The coastal town of Woolgoolga in northern New South Wales became home to a Punjabi farming community, and one of Australia's earliest gurdwaras was built there in the late 1960s.\n\nSikhism is now one of the fastest growing religions in the country, with more than 200,000 people identifying as Sikh in the 2021 census. Worldwide there are around 25 to 30 million Sikhs, most of them in Punjab. Many Australian gurdwaras cook langar for communities hit by bushfires and floods, carrying the free kitchen into new places."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do people sit together on the floor to eat langar?", ["It keeps the hall cleaner","It puts everyone at the same level","It is a rule for visitors only","It saves money on furniture"], 1, "Sitting side by side is deliberate: langar is a practical way of living out equality, not charity from above."],
    [1, "What does the Sikh practice of Kirat Karni ask of a person?", ["To fast each week","To make a pilgrimage","To earn an honest living","To avoid all possessions"], 2, "Kirat Karni, honest work, sits alongside remembering God and sharing with others as the core Sikh practices."],
    [2, "What did Guru Gobind Singh decide about the line of Gurus?", ["The scripture would be the eternal Guru","His eldest son would lead for life","A council would choose each Guru","The Golden Temple would become the Guru"], 0, "He ended the line of human Gurus, so the Guru Granth Sahib is treated as the living Guru."],
    [3, "Why do many Sikhs use the shared surnames Singh and Kaur?", ["They are required by law for all Sikhs in India","They show the trade a family has worked in","They mark the village a Sikh family came from","They reject caste, which many surnames revealed"], 3, "Shared surnames were another way of rejecting caste, since surnames in the region often revealed it."],
    [4, "How do many Australian gurdwaras carry the langar tradition today?", ["By running cooking classes for visiting tourists","By selling meals to raise money for the temple","By cooking free meals after bushfires and floods","By holding langar only on major festival days"], 2, "Cooking langar for disaster-hit communities extends the free kitchen beyond the gurdwara walls."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why a free hot meal is waiting at every Sikh temple, and what it says about equality."});

WB.L("shinto", "beliefs", "beginner", "Shinto: Japan's way of the kami",
  "Japan is dotted with more than 80,000 shrines, marked by bright red gates. Behind them sits a tradition with no founder, no single holy book and a world full of spirits.",
  [
    ["Gates and kami", "Shinto is Japan's native tradition of honouring kami, sacred powers found in nature, places and ancestors.",
      "A tall vermilion gate of two posts and two crossbeams stands at the edge of a forest. This is a torii, the boundary between the everyday world and sacred ground. Beyond it lies a Shinto shrine, home to a kami.\n\nShinto, 'the way of the kami', is Japan's oldest religious tradition. It has no founder, no single scripture and no fixed list of beliefs. Kami means a god, a spirit or simply something that inspires awe. One might live in Mount Fuji, an old tree, a waterfall or a boulder wrapped in a rope with paper streamers. Others are mythological figures or honoured ancestors. 'Eight million kami' means countless, not an exact number. Kami are neither all-powerful nor perfectly good, so Shinto is about keeping a respectful relationship with them."],
    ["Myths of sun and sea", "Japan's ancient chronicles tell how the islands were created and how laughter coaxed the sun goddess from her cave.",
      "Japan's oldest surviving books, the Kojiki (712) and Nihon Shoki (720), record its myths. The deities Izanagi and Izanami stir the ocean with a jewelled spear, and brine dripping from its tip forms the first island. They give birth to the islands of Japan and many kami. When Izanami dies, Izanagi follows her to the land of the dead and flees in horror. Washing himself clean, he gives rise to new kami, including the sun goddess Amaterasu.\n\nUpset by her storm god brother Susanoo's pranks, Amaterasu hides in a cave and the world goes dark. The other kami hang a mirror outside, and Ame no Uzume performs a comic dance that makes them roar with laughter. Curious, Amaterasu peeks out, sees her own radiance in the mirror and is drawn into the open. Light returns."],
    ["Purity at the shrine", "Shinto treats impurity as spiritual dirt that can be washed away, so visitors purify before approaching the kami.",
      "A central idea in Shinto is purity. Impurity, called kegare, is not quite the same as sin. It is more like spiritual dirt that comes from contact with death, illness or wrongdoing, and it can be washed away.\n\nAt the entrance to most shrines is a stone basin with wooden ladles. Visitors rinse their left hand, then their right, then their mouth. At the main hall they often toss a coin into a box, bow twice, clap twice, make a wish or prayer, and bow once more. Priests perform larger purification rituals, waving a wand of paper streamers. Visitors can buy omamori charms for luck or safe driving, and omikuji, paper fortunes tied to racks if the news is bad."],
    ["Rebuilt every twenty years", "Japan's most sacred shrine, at Ise, is rebuilt every twenty years because Shinto treats renewal as sacred.",
      "The Grand Shrine of Ise, dedicated to Amaterasu, is the most sacred site in Shinto. Its main buildings are made of plain cypress wood with thatched roofs. Every twenty years they are carefully rebuilt on a neighbouring plot, and the old ones are taken down. The most recent rebuild was in 2013.\n\nThe tradition has continued, with some interruptions, for well over a thousand years. It keeps carpentry skills alive across generations and expresses the idea that renewal and freshness are sacred. It also raises a puzzle: is a building that is constantly rebuilt very new or very old? Western ideas of preserving original materials do not quite fit, and visitors often find that question as memorable as the shrine itself."],
    ["Shinto in modern Japan", "Most Japanese people mix Shinto and Buddhist customs, and Shinto today is mainly festivals, seasonal rituals and respect for nature.",
      "Ask Japanese people whether they are religious and many will say no, yet most take part in Shinto customs. Millions visit shrines in the first days of the New Year, babies are often presented at a shrine, and weddings may follow Shinto rites. Funerals, however, are usually Buddhist.\n\nFor centuries Shinto and Buddhism were blended, until the government separated them in the 1860s. From then until 1945, a nationalist form called State Shinto promoted the emperor as divine. After the Second World War, Emperor Hirohito publicly rejected the idea that he was a living god, and Japan's new constitution separated religion and the state. Today Shinto is mostly about community festivals, seasonal rituals and respect for nature."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the phrase 'eight million kami' suggest?", ["An exact count of the spirits","That kami are countless","The number of shrines in Japan","The number of ancient emperors"], 1, "The phrase means countless: kami are found everywhere, in nature, places and ancestors."],
    [1, "How was Amaterasu drawn out of her cave?", ["Her brother apologised to her","An earthquake split the cave open","The emperor ordered her to leave","Laughter made her curious about the noise"], 3, "The kami's laughter at the comic dance made Amaterasu peek out, and the mirror drew her fully into the open."],
    [2, "How does Shinto understand impurity, or kegare?", ["As spiritual dirt that can be washed away","As a sin that cannot be forgiven","As a punishment sent by the kami","As a sign of a weak faith"], 0, "Kegare comes from contact with death, illness or wrongdoing, and rituals such as rinsing at the basin cleanse it."],
    [3, "Why is the Grand Shrine of Ise rebuilt every twenty years?", ["Termites steadily destroy the wooden frames","Each new emperor orders a shrine of his own","Floods damage the shrine every generation","Renewal and freshness are considered sacred"], 3, "Rebuilding keeps both the buildings and the craft skills fresh, expressing the Shinto idea that renewal is sacred."],
    [4, "How do most Japanese people treat Shinto and Buddhism today?", ["They must choose only one","They take part in both without conflict","They practise neither of them","They follow Buddhism on holidays"], 1, "Shrine visits and weddings are often Shinto, while funerals are usually Buddhist, and few see a contradiction."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A sacred shrine in Japan is demolished and rebuilt every twenty years. Why?"});

WB.L("aboriginal-dreaming", "beliefs", "intermediate", "The Dreaming: law, land and story",
  "For tens of thousands of years, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have carried knowledge in story, song and country. 'Dreamtime' barely begins to describe it.",
  [
    ["More than Dreamtime", "The Dreaming is a living system of law, belief and knowledge, and 'Dreamtime' is only a clumsy English label for it.",
      "Many Australians first meet Aboriginal spirituality through the word 'Dreamtime', imagined as a magical era long ago. That misses a great deal. The Dreaming links creation, law, kinship, land and daily responsibility. It explains how the world came to be, and it tells people how to live and how to care for country. Australia's First Peoples have lived here for at least 50,000 years. There is no single version: each nation has its own beliefs and words.\n\nAnthropologists popularised 'Dreamtime' in the late 1800s, translating an Arrernte word. Many Aboriginal people find it clumsy, because it suggests something imaginary when these beliefs describe what is most real. The Anangu speak of Tjukurpa, and the Warlpiri of Jukurrpa. In the 1950s W. E. H. Stanner called it an 'everywhen': ancestral events continue to shape the present."],
    ["Ancestors and country", "Ancestral beings shaped the land and remain present in it, so country is family, history and law at once.",
      "In many Dreaming accounts, ancestral beings travelled across a formless or sleeping land, and their actions created its features. A waterhole may mark where an ancestor rested, a ridge where one lay down, a rock formation where a battle took place.\n\nThese beings took many forms: animals, plants, people, stars, and powerful figures like the Rainbow Serpent. Its stories appear in different ways across much of the continent and are often tied to water and fertility.\n\nBecause ancestors are believed to remain present in the land, country is not just scenery or property. It is family, history and law all at once. Many Aboriginal people speak of belonging to country, rather than country belonging to them."],
    ["Songlines across country", "Songlines are sung routes across the land that carry stories, law and practical knowledge.",
      "The journeys of ancestral beings are often remembered as songlines, sometimes called Dreaming tracks. A songline can stretch hundreds or even thousands of kilometres, crossing the lands of many nations. Each group holds responsibility for its own section of the song.\n\nSinging the verses in order works like a map. It names waterholes, landmarks and food sources in sequence, which could help people travel safely through dry country. Songlines also carry law, kinship rules and seasonal knowledge, and some follow patterns in the stars. Researchers studying memory are keenly interested in how songs, places and stories together can hold huge amounts of information without writing. For the custodians, though, songlines are first and foremost sacred."],
    ["Not every story is shared", "Some Dreaming knowledge is restricted, and sharing it needs the right authority.",
      "A key point for outsiders is that Dreaming knowledge is not public property. Some stories are open, often told to children and visitors. Others are restricted to people who have gone through certain ceremonies, or are known as men's business or women's business and kept within those groups. Certain sites are sacred and should not be photographed or entered.\n\nRights to tell a story, paint its designs or sing its songs belong to particular custodians, who inherit that responsibility through kinship. This is why respectful books, galleries and tourism operators work with traditional owners and seek permission. It is also why this lesson does not retell specific sacred stories: the respectful way to hear them is from the people who hold them."],
    ["A living tradition", "The Dreaming is not in the past: it guides decisions like the Uluru climb closure and lives on in ceremony, art and language.",
      "At Uluru, Tjukurpa is present for the Anangu traditional owners, and the climbing route has deep spiritual significance. For decades signs asked tourists not to climb, and many did anyway. After the park was handed back to Anangu ownership in 1985 and leased to the national parks agency, the joint management board voted to end the climb. It closed permanently in October 2019. Visitors now walk around the base, where guides share stories that are open to the public.\n\nColonisation caused enormous harm, including dispossession, language loss and the removal of children. Yet ceremony, song and story survive, and communities are reviving languages. Since the 1970s, painters from Papunya in the Western Desert have shared permitted aspects of their Dreamings through acrylic art. When you hear a Welcome to Country, you hear a very old relationship with land."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do many Aboriginal people find 'Dreamtime' a clumsy translation?", ["It suggests something imaginary, not real","It was invented by a single nation","It refers only to children's stories","It is too old-fashioned to use"], 0, "The Dreaming is a living framework of law and belief, not a fantasy confined to the distant past."],
    [1, "What does it mean to say Aboriginal people belong to country?", ["They are legally tied to one house","They must never leave their home","Country is family, history and law","They own land in the European sense"], 2, "Because ancestors remain present in the land, country carries kinship, history and responsibility."],
    [2, "What is one practical function of a songline?", ["It records colonial property boundaries","It names landmarks and waterholes in order","It sets the dates of ceremonies each year","It counts how many people live nearby"], 1, "Sung in order, a songline names places and resources along a route, helping people navigate dry country."],
    [3, "Why might a respectful book avoid retelling certain Dreaming stories?", ["Every nation tells exactly the same stories","Most of the stories have been lost over time","Some knowledge belongs to particular custodians","Stories about country are only meant for children"], 2, "Rights to share a story belong to specific custodians, so the respectful way to hear one is from them."],
    [4, "What guided the decision to close the Uluru climb in 2019?", ["A government plan to build a new road","Anangu law and the site's spiritual meaning","A rockfall that made it unsafe to climb","Advice from a tourism marketing review"], 1, "For the Anangu, Tjukurpa makes the route sacred, and the joint management board voted to end the climb."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why 'Dreamtime' is an English word that barely begins to describe Aboriginal law and land."});

WB.L("epic-of-gilgamesh", "beliefs", "intermediate", "Gilgamesh: the world's oldest great story",
  "A tyrant king, a wild man from the steppe and a desperate quest to cheat death. Written on clay over 3,000 years ago, Gilgamesh still feels startlingly modern.",
  [
    ["Clay that kept a secret", "Gilgamesh was rediscovered on clay tablets from Nineveh, and its hero may have been a real king of Uruk.",
      "In 1872 George Smith, a young assistant at the British Museum, was piecing together broken clay tablets from the ruins of Nineveh in modern Iraq. In the cuneiform script he found a flood, a man who built a boat and a bird sent to look for land. It looked strikingly like Noah's story. The tablets came from the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal, from the 600s BCE, and held the Epic of Gilgamesh.\n\nGilgamesh was probably based on a real king of Uruk, one of the first great cities, who may have reigned around 2700 BCE, though there is no direct proof. He grew into a legend, two thirds god and one third human. Sumerian poems about him came first, around 2100 BCE, and were later woven into a Babylonian epic of twelve tablets."],
    ["Tyrant and wild man", "The gods create Enkidu to balance a proud Gilgamesh, and their friendship leads them into heroic deeds that cost a life.",
      "Gilgamesh is magnificent but arrogant, and he oppresses the people of Uruk. The gods answer their cries by creating Enkidu, a wild man who lives among the animals of the steppe. Shamhat brings him into human society, and he learns to eat bread, drink beer and wear clothes. When Enkidu confronts Gilgamesh, the two wrestle fiercely. Neither wins, and the struggle becomes a deep friendship.\n\nSeeking lasting fame, they travel to the Cedar Forest and kill its guardian, Humbaba, with help from the sun god Shamash. The goddess Ishtar proposes marriage to Gilgamesh, and he rudely refuses. She sends the Bull of Heaven to ravage the land, and the heroes slay it too. The gods take note: one of the friends must die for these offences, and the lot falls on Enkidu."],
    ["Grief and fear of death", "Enkidu's death makes Gilgamesh face his own mortality, and the epic becomes a meditation on it.",
      "Enkidu falls ill and dies after days of suffering, and Gilgamesh is shattered. He refuses to let his friend be buried until, in a famously grim detail, a worm falls from Enkidu's nose. The mighty king suddenly understands that he too will die.\n\nThis is the emotional heart of the epic, and it is why Gilgamesh feels so human more than 3,000 years later. He tears off his fine clothes, dresses in animal skins and wanders the wilderness, consumed by grief and terror. His question is one every reader recognises: is there any way to escape death? He sets out to find the one man said to have been granted eternal life."],
    ["The flood survivor", "Utnapishtim survived a flood sent by the gods and was granted immortality, a story that parallels the biblical flood.",
      "After a long journey, including crossing the waters of death, Gilgamesh reaches Utnapishtim, a wise man who lives with his wife at the edge of the world. Utnapishtim tells his story. The gods decided to destroy humanity with a flood, but the god Ea secretly warned him to build a huge boat and load it with his family, craftsmen and animals.\n\nThe storm raged for days. When it ended, the boat came to rest on a mountain. Utnapishtim released a dove, a swallow and then a raven, which did not return because it had found land. Grateful, he made an offering, and the gods granted him and his wife immortality. Scholars still discuss how this story relates to the biblical flood."],
    ["The plant and the walls", "Gilgamesh cannot cheat death, but the epic suggests our work, cities and stories can outlast us.",
      "Utnapishtim challenges Gilgamesh to stay awake for a week, and the exhausted king immediately falls asleep. Out of kindness, Utnapishtim tells him of a plant at the bottom of the sea that restores youth. Gilgamesh ties stones to his feet, dives down and gets it.\n\nOn the way home, while he bathes in a pool, a snake slithers up and eats the plant, shedding its skin as it goes. Gilgamesh weeps. He returns to Uruk empty-handed, and the epic ends with him showing a companion the city's mighty walls and brickwork. The message many readers take is that we cannot live forever, but our work, our cities and our stories can outlast us."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do many historians view Gilgamesh?", ["As a god who was invented from nothing","As a Greek hero later adopted by Assyria","As a character first written in the 1800s","As a legend likely based on a real king"], 3, "King lists name Gilgamesh as a ruler of Uruk, so he is part history, part myth and part great literature."],
    [1, "Why do the gods decide that Enkidu must die?", ["He killed Humbaba and the Bull of Heaven","He refused to leave the steppe","He stole the plant of youth from the sea","He broke a promise to the goddess Ishtar"], 0, "The heroes' killing of the Cedar Forest guardian and the Bull of Heaven offended the gods."],
    [2, "What sends Gilgamesh on his quest for eternal life?", ["A prophecy from the goddess Ishtar","A command from the council of gods","Grief for Enkidu and fear of his own death","A dream about a coming flood"], 2, "Enkidu's death forces the king to face his own mortality, which is the emotional heart of the epic."],
    [3, "What did the raven's failure to return show Utnapishtim?", ["That the bird had died at sea","That it had found dry land","That the gods were angry","That the flood was starting again"], 1, "A bird that did not come back meant the waters had gone down enough for it to find land."],
    [4, "What message do many readers take from the ending of the epic?", ["The gods always reward the brave","Youth can be restored by diving deep","Our work, cities and stories can outlast us","Kings should never travel"], 2, "Gilgamesh cannot live forever, but the walls of Uruk stand as a legacy that endures."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A tablet found in 1872 told a flood story older than the Bible's. Who was its hero?"});

WB.L("flood-myths", "beliefs", "advanced", "The great flood stories",
  "Noah's ark is famous, but it is one of many. From Mesopotamia to India, Greece and China, cultures have told of a world drowned and begun again.",
  [
    ["Why floods make stories", "Flood stories appear across cultures because floods were a shared fear, and a fresh start gives a story room for big questions.",
      "Imagine rain that does not stop, rivers that swallow fields, then a single boat afloat on an endless sea. Versions of this image appear around the world: Noah's ark in the Hebrew Bible, flood heroes in Mesopotamia, a warning fish in Hindu tradition, and Greek, Chinese and many Indigenous stories. For believers, some carry deep religious meaning. For historians, the pattern raises questions about shared memories and borrowed stories.\n\nEarly civilisations grew along rivers and coasts, where floods threatened lives yet fed fertile soil. A flood also wipes the slate clean, so it lets a tradition ask who deserves to survive and why. The survivors start a new world, so the stories are as much about hope as destruction."],
    ["Mesopotamia's floods", "The oldest written flood stories come from ancient Mesopotamia, where river floods were a very real fear.",
      "The earliest known written flood stories come from Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers in modern Iraq. A Sumerian account tells of a king named Ziusudra who survives a flood.\n\nA Babylonian poem, the Atrahasis epic, explains that the gods sent the flood because humans had become too numerous and noisy, disturbing their rest. The wise Atrahasis is secretly warned by the god Enki and builds a boat. The same story appears in the Epic of Gilgamesh, where the survivor is called Utnapishtim.\n\nThese tablets date back to roughly the early second millennium BCE. On flat land beside bursting rivers, a catastrophic flood was a very real fear."],
    ["Noah and the parallels", "Genesis and the Mesopotamian accounts share a shape but differ in message: moral judgment and a promise.",
      "In Genesis, shared by Judaism and Christianity, with a version in the Quran where Noah is called Nuh, God sees the earth filled with violence and sends a flood. The righteous Noah survives with his family and pairs of animals. Afterwards he sends out a raven and a dove, and the dove returns with an olive leaf. God makes a covenant never again to destroy the earth by flood, with the rainbow as its sign.\n\nWhen George Smith translated the Gilgamesh flood tablet in 1872, readers saw close parallels: a divine warning, a great boat, animals, a landing on a mountain, birds and a sacrifice. Many scholars think both draw on a shared regional tradition. But in Mesopotamia many quarrelsome gods act for seemingly petty reasons. In Genesis one God responds to wrongdoing and ends with a promise."],
    ["Fish, stones and dams", "Hindu, Greek and Chinese flood stories each add their own twist.",
      "In Hindu tradition, the first man, Manu, finds a tiny fish that asks for protection. He cares for it as it grows huge, and it turns out to be the god Vishnu in his fish form, Matsya. The fish warns of a flood and tows Manu's boat to safety.\n\nIn Greek myth, Deucalion and his wife Pyrrha survive a flood sent by Zeus, then repopulate the world by throwing stones over their shoulders, which become people.\n\nChinese tradition tells of Yu the Great, who succeeded where his father had failed. Instead of building dams to block the water, Yu dug channels to guide it to the sea. The Chinese story is less about divine punishment and more about wise human effort."],
    ["Memories of rising seas", "Some flood stories may preserve real memories of coastlines drowned as seas rose after the last Ice Age.",
      "Could some flood stories remember real events? At the end of the last Ice Age, melting ice sheets raised global sea levels by well over 100 metres, a process that largely ended around 7,000 years ago. Coastlines around the world moved inland.\n\nIn Australia, researchers including linguist Nicholas Reid and geographer Patrick Nunn have studied Aboriginal stories from many coastal areas that describe the sea rising and land being lost. They argue that some may preserve memories from that time. Others have proposed that a sudden flooding of the Black Sea inspired ancient legends, though that idea remains debated.\n\nThese theories are interesting but not settled, and none of them proves or disproves any particular religious account."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a flood make such a useful story setting?", ["It resets the world, so big questions can be asked","It was the only disaster early peoples feared","It always proves that a historical event occurred","It was borrowed from one single ancient culture"], 0, "A flood resets the world, so stories can explore why bad things happen and who deserves to survive."],
    [1, "According to the Atrahasis epic, why did the gods send a flood?", ["Humans had stopped making sacrifices","A king had insulted the sun god","Humans had become too numerous and noisy","The rivers needed refilling"], 2, "In Atrahasis, the gods are disturbed by the noise of an overpopulated humanity."],
    [2, "What is a key difference between Genesis and the Mesopotamian flood accounts?", ["Only Genesis has a boat that lands on a mountain","Genesis has one God, wrongdoing and a promise","Only the Mesopotamian versions mention animals","Genesis has no birds, unlike Mesopotamian tales"], 1, "Mesopotamian versions feature many gods with sometimes petty motives, while Genesis centres on one God, wrongdoing and a covenant."],
    [3, "How did Yu the Great tame the flood in Chinese tradition?", ["By building much taller dams","By praying to a dragon king","By building a huge wooden ark","By digging channels to guide the water"], 3, "Yu succeeded by channelling the water, where his father's dams had failed."],
    [4, "What might some Aboriginal coastal stories remember, according to researchers?", ["A tsunami from a distant volcano","Seas rising after the last Ice Age","A comet striking the ocean","A great flood of the Murray"], 1, "Researchers argue some stories may preserve memories of coastlines lost as seas rose after the last Ice Age."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Many cultures tell of a drowned world and a fresh start. What are they remembering?"});

WB.L("zoroastrianism", "beliefs", "advanced", "Zoroastrianism: the fire and the choice",
  "Once the faith of Persian empires, now held by a small community, Zoroastrianism asks every person to pick a side in a cosmic battle between good and evil.",
  [
    ["The flame and the prophet", "Zoroastrians pray in the presence of fire as a symbol of purity, and the faith traces back to the prophet Zarathustra.",
      "In the Iranian city of Yazd, a flame burns in a large metal vessel inside a fire temple. According to the community, it has been kept alight for many centuries by priests who add sandalwood and recite prayers. Outsiders sometimes call Zoroastrians 'fire worshippers', but that is a misunderstanding. Fire is a symbol of purity, light and the divine, and prayers are said in its presence rather than to it.\n\nThe faith takes its name from Zarathustra, known to the Greeks as Zoroaster. Scholars disagree about when he lived, from around 1500 BCE to about 600 BCE. His hymns, the Gathas, are the oldest part of the Avesta, the Zoroastrian scripture. In them he speaks directly to God, asking about justice, truth and how to live."],
    ["Wise Lord and free choice", "Zoroastrians see a world split between good and evil, and every person freely chooses a side.",
      "Zoroastrians worship one supreme God, Ahura Mazda, the Wise Lord, who is entirely good. Opposed to him is Angra Mainyu, the destructive spirit, the source of evil, sickness and death. The world is a struggle between asha, truth and right order, and druj, the lie. Scholars debate whether this is dualistic or monotheistic, but Zoroastrians generally call their faith monotheistic. Evil is not part of God's nature, and it will ultimately be defeated.\n\nBecause the world is a battleground, every choice counts. People are not born sinful. They have free will and strengthen the good through honest, kind and hard-working lives and care for earth, water and fire. The motto is good thoughts, good words, good deeds. Young people are initiated at the navjote, receiving a sacred cord, the kusti, which many retie several times a day with prayers."],
    ["The bridge of judgment", "Zoroastrian tradition teaches judgment after death and a final renewal of the world, ideas that may have shaped other faiths.",
      "Zoroastrianism has a vivid picture of what happens after death. Tradition teaches that on the third night after a person dies, the soul crosses the Chinvat Bridge. For the righteous, the bridge is wide and they pass to the House of Song, a paradise. For the wicked it narrows like a blade, and they fall into a place of suffering.\n\nAt the end of time, a saviour figure called the Saoshyant will appear, the dead will be raised, evil will be defeated and the world will be renewed in perfection. Many scholars think these ideas of individual judgment, heaven and hell, resurrection and a final saviour influenced Judaism, Christianity and Islam, though the extent of that influence is still debated."],
    ["Faith of empires", "Zoroastrianism was tied to Persian power for over a thousand years, then shrank to a minority after the Arab conquest.",
      "Zoroastrian beliefs were bound up with Persian power for over a thousand years. Inscriptions of the Achaemenid king Darius the Great, who ruled from about 522 BCE, credit his rule to Ahuramazda, a form of Ahura Mazda. The Sasanian empire, from the 200s CE, made Zoroastrianism its official religion, with a powerful priesthood and grand fire temples.\n\nMany people believe the Magi who visit the infant Jesus in the Gospel of Matthew were Zoroastrian priests, as magi was a term for that priestly class, though the Gospel does not say so directly. After the Arab conquest of Persia in the 600s CE, Islam gradually became the majority faith, and Zoroastrians became a shrinking minority in their homeland. Today they number only in the low hundreds of thousands worldwide."],
    ["The Parsis today", "Parsis, descendants of Zoroastrians who migrated to India, are a small but influential community.",
      "According to tradition, a group of Zoroastrians left Persia and sailed to the coast of Gujarat in India, perhaps around the 700s to 900s CE, seeking freedom to practise their faith. Their descendants are called Parsis, meaning Persians. Many later settled in Mumbai, where Parsis became prominent in business, law and philanthropy. The Tata family is a famous example, and the rock star Freddie Mercury, born Farrokh Bulsara, came from a Parsi family.\n\nTraditionally Parsis placed the dead in Towers of Silence to be exposed to vultures, to avoid polluting earth or fire, though this is changing. The community is small and ageing, and debates about intermarriage and conversion are lively. Iran's festival of Nowruz has Zoroastrian roots."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do Zoroastrians understand their sacred fire?", ["As a god to be worshipped","As a weapon against evil spirits","As a way to cook offerings","As a symbol of purity and the divine"], 3, "Fire symbolises purity, light and divine presence; prayers are said before it, not to it."],
    [1, "Why does every choice matter in Zoroastrian teaching?", ["People are born sinful and must be saved","People freely choose a side in the struggle","Fate has already decided every outcome","Only priests can influence the outcome"], 1, "People have free will, so honest, kind actions strengthen the side of good in the struggle against evil."],
    [2, "What happens at the Chinvat Bridge, according to tradition?", ["The sacred fire is first lit","Souls are judged after death","Young people are initiated","Kings are crowned"], 1, "The bridge widens for the righteous and narrows like a blade for the wicked."],
    [3, "How did Zoroastrianism's position change after the 600s CE?", ["It spread across Europe as a major faith","It was replaced by the Greek religion","It grew stronger as Persia was conquered","It shrank from an empire faith to a minority"], 3, "After the Arab conquest, Islam became the majority faith, leaving Zoroastrians a minority in their homeland."],
    [4, "Who are the Parsis?", ["Descendants of Zoroastrians who moved to India","Priests who tend the fire temples of Iran","A medieval Persian dynasty of Muslim rulers","Followers of a breakaway Islamic sect in Iran"], 0, "Parsis descend from Zoroastrians who, by tradition, sailed from Persia to Gujarat."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Zoroastrians pray beside fire, not to it. What do they see in the flame?"});

WB.L("daoism", "beliefs", "beginner", "Daoism: the way of water",
  "A short ancient book, a sage who may never have existed and the idea that doing less can achieve more. Daoism has shaped Chinese life for over 2,000 years.",
  [
    ["The way of water", "Daoism uses water as an image of quiet strength and points to the Dao, the unnameable pattern behind all things.",
      "Watch a river carve a canyon. Water does not fight the rock. It flows around it and, over time, wears down stone. The Daodejing, Daoism's most famous text, praises water again and again: it benefits all things without competing, and nothing can withstand it.\n\nDaoism is one of China's great traditions, alongside Confucianism and Buddhism, and is both a philosophy and an organised religion. Dao means 'way'. It is the source of the universe and the natural pattern by which it unfolds. The Daodejing opens by saying the Dao that can be spoken of is not the eternal Dao: words can point towards it but never capture it. The Dao is no god issuing commands. It is more like the grain of the universe. The wise person learns to recognise its patterns and move with them."],
    ["Laozi and the gatekeeper", "Tradition credits Laozi with the Daodejing, but most historians think it was compiled from earlier sayings.",
      "Tradition credits the Daodejing to Laozi, whose name means 'Old Master'. A famous legend says he was a record keeper at the royal court of the Zhou dynasty. Weary of the decline of society, he rode west on a water buffalo to leave China. At the border pass, a gatekeeper recognised his wisdom and begged him to write down his teachings.\n\nLaozi wrote a short book of about five thousand characters, handed it over and was never seen again.\n\nMost historians today think the Daodejing was compiled from earlier sayings, probably around the 300s or 400s BCE, and that Laozi may be partly or wholly legendary. The book is now one of the most translated texts in the world."],
    ["Wu wei", "Wu wei means acting in harmony with circumstances, effortlessly and without strain, not doing nothing.",
      "One of Daoism's central ideas is wu wei, often translated as 'non-action' but better understood as effortless or natural action. It does not mean being lazy. It means acting in harmony with circumstances rather than forcing them, like a skilled swimmer who uses the current instead of thrashing against it.\n\nA story from the Zhuangzi, another key Daoist text, describes a cook who carves an ox so skilfully that his knife never dulls, because he follows the natural spaces between the joints. Daoist writing applies the same idea to rulers: the best leaders govern so lightly that people barely notice them. Modern ideas about being in the 'flow' echo this ancient thought."],
    ["The butterfly dream", "Zhuangzi's playful stories invite us to hold our fixed ideas about reality lightly.",
      "The second great Daoist thinker, Zhuangzi, lived around the 300s BCE and wrote with humour and imagination. In his most famous passage, he dreams he is a butterfly, fluttering happily with no idea he is Zhuangzi. Then he wakes and is unmistakably himself. But now he wonders: was he Zhuangzi dreaming of being a butterfly, or a butterfly now dreaming of being Zhuangzi?\n\nThe story is less a riddle to solve than an invitation to hold our fixed ideas lightly. Zhuangzi loved stories that turn conventional values upside down: useless trees that survive because no carpenter wants them, and outcasts who turn out to be wiser than kings. His book remains one of the most enjoyable works of ancient philosophy."],
    ["Yin, yang and daily life", "Yin and yang are complementary forces, and Daoist ideas live on in religion, tai chi and popular culture.",
      "Yin, the dark, receptive side, and yang, the bright, active side, are not good and evil but complementary forces, each holding a seed of the other. The idea is older than Daoism, yet the yin yang symbol is one of its emblems.\n\nAround the 100s CE, Daoism developed as an organised religion, with priests, temples, festivals and immortals such as the Eight Immortals. Some Daoists sought long life through meditation, breathing, diet and alchemy, and some elixirs contained toxic mercury, with deadly results.\n\nTai chi, practised in parks around the world, draws on Daoist ideas about balance and qi. Traditional Chinese medicine and feng shui share similar ideas. In China, people often blend Confucian, Buddhist and Daoist teachings. The core message travels well: slow down, notice nature and stop forcing things."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the Daodejing keep returning to the image of water?", ["It shows gentle strength that wears down stone","It warns about the danger of floods","It stands for the wealth of the emperor","It represents the cycle of the seasons"], 0, "Water benefits all things without competing, and over time nothing can withstand it."],
    [1, "What do most historians think about the Daodejing?", ["It was written in one night at a border pass","It was compiled from earlier sayings","It was written by Confucius himself","It was copied from an Indian text"], 1, "The Laozi story is a famous legend, but the book was likely assembled from older material."],
    [2, "What is the best description of wu wei?", ["Refusing to work at all","Strict obedience to rulers","Fasting for a long life","Acting naturally, without strain"], 3, "Wu wei is effortless action, like the cook whose knife follows the natural spaces in the ox."],
    [3, "What is the point of Zhuangzi's butterfly dream?", ["To prove that dreams predict the future","To invite us to hold our fixed ideas lightly","To argue that waking life is worthless","To warn that dreaming is dangerous"], 1, "The story questions how certain we can be about reality rather than offering a puzzle with an answer."],
    [4, "How do Daoists view yin and yang?", ["As good and evil locked in permanent war","As two rival gods competing for worship","As complementary forces that keep balance","As heaven and hell in the afterlife"], 2, "Yin and yang are not moral opposites but complementary forces."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A sage who may never have existed, and a book that says doing less can achieve more."});

WB.L("maui-polynesian-myths", "beliefs", "beginner", "Māui and the myths of Polynesia",
  "He fished up islands, slowed the sun and stole fire. Māui, the trickster demigod, is a hero across thousands of kilometres of Pacific Ocean.",
  [
    ["A hero of the Pacific", "Māui is a clever, underestimated demigod whose stories are told across Polynesia, from Aotearoa to Hawaiʻi.",
      "Picture the vast triangle of Polynesia. It runs from Aotearoa New Zealand in the south-west to Hawaiʻi in the north and Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, in the east. Island peoples with shared ancestral roots tell stories of the same mischievous hero: Māui, a clever, cheeky, slightly reckless demigod whose adventures explain features of the world.\n\nIn a well-known telling, Māui was born early and his mother, Taranga, wrapped him in her topknot and cast him into the sea. Seaweed and jellyfish protected him, and a sky ancestor raised him. As a boy he returned to announce himself to his surprised family. He begins as an outsider who must prove himself, as the youngest of several brothers."],
    ["Fishing up a land", "Māori tradition says Māui hauled up the North Island like a giant fish, using a magical hook.",
      "The most famous Māui story in Aotearoa tells how he hid in the bottom of his brothers' canoe when they went fishing. Far out to sea, he revealed himself and lowered a magical hook made from the jawbone of his grandmother, Murirangawhenua. He caught something enormous and hauled it up: a vast fish, which became the North Island.\n\nIts Māori name, Te Ika a Māui, means the fish of Māui. His brothers began hacking at the fish before it had settled, which is said to explain the North Island's mountains and valleys. The South Island is often known as Te Waka a Māui, his canoe, and Stewart Island as Te Punga a Māui, its anchor stone."],
    ["Sun and fire", "Māui slowed the sun to lengthen the day and tricked the fire goddess, leaving fire hidden in certain trees.",
      "The sun once raced across the sky so fast that people lacked daylight to fish, farm and cook. Māui and his brothers plaited strong ropes, went to where the sun rose and snared it. Māui beat it with his grandmother's jawbone until it agreed to travel more slowly. In Hawaiian tradition, a similar feat happens on Haleakalā, 'house of the sun', on Maui.\n\nIn another story, Māui put out the village fires and visited the fire goddess Mahuika, who kept fire in her fingernails. He tricked her into giving up nail after nail until one was left. She threw it at him, setting the land ablaze. Māui called on rain, and the last sparks escaped into certain trees, such as the kaikōmako. That is why rubbing sticks of those woods together makes fire."],
    ["The death of a trickster", "Māui's bid to win immortality for humans fails, and the story asks listeners to accept mortality.",
      "Māui's final adventure is his most daring. He wanted to defeat death itself, so that people could live forever. Death was personified by Hine nui te pō, the great woman of the night, a guardian of the underworld.\n\nMāui planned to crawl through her body while she slept, which would reverse the course of death. He took birds with him and warned them to keep quiet. But as he crawled, the sight was so comical that a little fantail, the pīwakawaka, burst into laughter. Hine nui te pō woke and crushed Māui, and so all people remain mortal.\n\nLike Gilgamesh, the hero fails to escape death, and the story asks listeners to accept mortality as part of life."],
    ["Stories still alive", "Polynesian myths are living culture, tied to Māori genealogy, the new year of Matariki and debates about how they are portrayed.",
      "These stories are not museum pieces. Many Māori trace their whakapapa, or genealogy, back through gods and ancestors, so myths and family history are deeply connected. Māori creation traditions describe the sky father Ranginui and earth mother Papatūānuku, pushed apart by their son Tāne to let light into the world.\n\nMatariki, the Māori new year marked by the rising of the Pleiades star cluster, became a New Zealand public holiday in 2022. Māui reached a global audience in Disney's 2016 film Moana. Many people enjoyed it, while some Pacific voices criticised the way Māui was portrayed and argued that sacred figures should be depicted with care. That debate shows how much these stories still matter to the people who own them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What pattern do Māui's stories share?", ["An underestimated outsider proves himself","A king defeats a rival island","A farmer is rewarded for patience","A priest is tested by the gods"], 0, "Māui begins as an overlooked outsider, so his triumphs are all the sweeter."],
    [1, "How does the story explain the North Island's mountains and valleys?", ["A volcano shaped the fish when it landed","The fish was dragged across the sea floor","The hook tore deep gashes into the land","His brothers hacked at the fish too soon"], 3, "The brothers cut into the great fish before it settled, creating its mountains and valleys."],
    [2, "Why can people make fire by rubbing sticks of certain woods?", ["The last sparks hid in certain trees","The sun gave fire to the forest","Māui buried fire beneath the trees","Rain trapped fire in the soil"], 0, "When Māui called rain to quench the flames, the final sparks hid in trees such as the kaikōmako."],
    [3, "What does Māui's failure to defeat death ask listeners to do?", ["Look harder for a way to cheat death","Avoid trying to trick the gods","Accept mortality as part of life","Treat the night with greater fear"], 2, "Like Gilgamesh, Māui cannot escape death, and the story invites acceptance of it."],
    [4, "Why are Māori myths described as living culture?", ["They are mainly kept in written archives","They are performed only for visitors","They have been replaced by newer tales","They link to Māori genealogy and identity"], 3, "Myth and family history are connected, so the stories remain part of identity today."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"He fished up an island and slowed the sun. How did a trickster end up mortal?"});

